A||N||6||JeffDm||Budget sound recommendations?||||5||
Z||000000||JeffDm||04-13-2001||11:45 PM||||I'm relatively new to Fieros.  I tried the search but it didn't cut it for the info I wanted.<P>I have an '88 car, the body style name escapes me but it's the one that's a  throwback to older fieros, non-GT I believe.<P>If I decide to keep the car I would like some basic recommendations on what to look for.<P>Basically the stock stereo/cassette unit functions, but it's, well, stock and it is POS Delco.  The family has 5 GM cars, all built in the 80's and only 1 Delco stereo works and it's mine, so I have a track record to justify its tossing, plus I want an upgrade from cassette.<P>I would like a CD or MD player that also has an "auxiliary in" jack so I can hook up a portable MD or CD player when I want to.  Does anyone make this feature?  I haven't found it but I haven't been poking around every display I see.<P>Then there's the faceplate adaptor - are there any good looking ones?  The systems I see that fill out the dash hole are too expensive that I have found, but I don't like a lot of the adaptors that I've seen either.<P>I would like to know what to look for in speakers, I don't want to modify any parts though.  What info do I need to look for specific shapes.  Also, how does one get to the speaker panels on the dash, on a short try I couldn't find an easy way to get to it.  Do I have to remove the entire dash cover?<P>I can't do a sub for the time being, I don't want to spend more than a total $500 for the starting package.  Basically I appreciate good sound but I have a budget and I want to get the awful speakers out of there ASAP, I think the owner said they've been "upgraded" but it's a dubious upgrade.<P>I appreciate any good input you can provide.<p>[This message has been edited by JeffDm (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||04-14-2001||12:06 AM||||A good start for your system would be new 4x10s for the dash. For $80-100 you can get a set of Pioneer or Kenwood 4x10s. I have the Pioneers and am very pleased with them. Decent bass response, especially if you spend a little more for an amp. For $70 I bought a Phase Linear amp at Circuit City. Allegdly 200w, but for the price, it drives the Pioneers well.<BR>Also, don't cheap out on your head unit. Get one that will support adding on extra amps and has all the whistles and bells you are looking for. I recently installed a Pioneer DEH-P77 DH. Great unit. MOSFET power supply, 45wx4. Has front, rear, and subwoofer RCA outputs, plus a built-in alarm. Better yet, it is DIN & 1/2, so it fits perfectly in stock opening, no kit needed. It will also control other Pioneer products like CD changer, TV tuner, etc. I got mine for $230 including shipping from <A HREF="http://www.competitionsound.com" TARGET=_blank>www.competitionsound.com</A> <P>To replace dash speakers, gently pry up on the corners of the speaker grill with a flat tip screwdriver. The speakers drop right in from the top. Rear speakers are more of a job, as it entails removing the seatbelts and taking out that entire panel. THE GRILLS ON THE REAR DO NOT COME OFF! Well, unless you break them off, and you don't wanna do that.<P>Anyhow, welcome to the forum. Let me know when you are ready to add your subwoofer. I make a spiffy enclosure for the passenger footwell. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||04-14-2001||12:52 AM||||You can get a Sony MD player and the installation kit from crutchfield. It doesn't have an auxillary input, but it will also control a CD changer if you need it. It looks like this installed:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1629836&a=12458590&p=45776424&Sequence=0&res=high">  <P>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-14-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jake2m4||04-15-2001||01:19 PM||||Hey, I know AIWA makes a cd player with an INPUT jack on the front.  It costs about 120 at best buy.  I bought the tape deck version ($80 - 90) for my Grand Am (since it had a cd changer already)  I use the input for my mp3 player and it works great.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||04-15-2001||01:49 PM||||OK here you go:<BR>Aiwa CDC-X407<BR>AM/FM/CD. Has aux input in front for CD/Cassette/MD and also plays CD-R and CD-RW with MP3 files.<BR>Output is 35W X 4<BR>Detachable face for security<BR>Wireless remote<BR>Multi-color display<BR>Source level adjustment (you can control the input to the powered sub from the stereo)<BR>cost $169<P>(If you don't want the detachable face, Multi color display, and wireless remote you can get the CDC-Z107 for $129. All other features are the same)<P>Blaupunkt PC-X462 <BR>4X6 2-way speaker (for sail panels)<BR>cost $59.00<P>Pioneer TS-A403<BR>4X10 2-way speaker (For Dash)<BR>cost $99.00<P>Bazooka EL6A-HP<BR>6 1/2" powered sub<BR>Powered with an internal 50W amp<BR>Mounts behind passenger seat and takes up little room. Sounds great with a basic system.<BR>Cost $199<P>All these prices are from Crutchfield.<P>Total cost os $527.00 + $4.95 shipping<BR>They will also send you ALL of the harness adapters and faceplate adapter for the entire system (radio and speakers) + they will send you drawings on how everything comes apart to install the system yourself.<P>Some of the above items can be bought at other places for less but at Crutchfield you get all the adapters free, and shipping is only $4.95 since you are buying a whole system.<BR>Without the powered sub its $326 + $4.95.<BR>With CDC-Z107 and sub = $487 + $4.95 <P>OOPS I forgot, If you get the sub you will need to buy a set of RCA cables. This would be to hook up the sub to the stereo. Most local stores sell them for $10-$20 depending on length and quality.<P><BR>Hope this helps.<P><BR>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-15-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||saleenfiero||04-15-2001||02:38 PM||||I think the only way you're gonna be able to hook up a portable CD/MD, is with a cassette deck and a discman tape adapter.  I used to use those and they do sound good.  Pain to adjust and hook up all the time though.<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||13
Z||000006||AkursedX||04-15-2001||03:03 PM||||I like Orief's idea for a basic setup.  The only thing I would say is to look around to other places besides Crutchfield.  I don't doubt their quality and their customer service one bit, but their prices are EXPENSIVE!!  If you do some searching around on ebay and on the rest of the web, you can find yourself some real good deals.  <P>I've got 4-inch components from kenwood in the front for $80 (ebay)<BR>Blaupunkt 4x6's in the rear, $42 (ebay)<BR>Panasonic CQ-DFX 801U CD  (It has an Auxillary input as well, but this is the top of the line Panasonic reciever and I'm sure you can find something cheaper)  $305  (ebay)<BR>Pioneer Preimer 10inch subwoofer  $74 (ebay)<BR>Amp wiring kit $30 (Crutchfield)<BR>Patch Cables $30 (Circuit City)<BR>Stimpy's Subwoofer Enclosure $150<BR>Amp 200x2 Boss Amp $60 (Bought from a friend)<P>So with just the reciever and the speakers, my total comes up to $427, and with the subwoofer and amp setup, it comes to $871.  So just do some searching around and I'm sure you can find what you need for a good price.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Edit:  oh, I forgot to add about $20-30 for all the harnesses.  I had some of mine already and the rest I just picked up from walmart.  Although I do admit that the adapter face for my reciever is ugly, I hope to find a different one.  <p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 04-15-2001).]||||reg||14
